rt2x00: add optional monitor-mode channel lock (RT3070/rt2800usb channel drift)

rt2800usb (Ralink RT2870/RT3070/RT35xx USB) is a non-channel-context driver:
mac80211 emulates channel contexts on its behalf and pushes every hardware
channel change down through a single callback, rt2x00mac_config(), as
IEEE80211_CONF_CHANGE_CHANNEL. That path is rt2x00mac_config() ->
rt2x00lib_config() -> rt2800_config_channel().

Because .config is the ONLY runtime path that retunes the radio for this
hardware, any off-channel activity mac80211 schedules while a monitor
interface is "locked" to a fixed channel necessarily arrives here as a
channel change and moves the radio. Sources include software/background scan,
channel-context reassignment when another vif (for example a leftover managed
wlanX left UP) wants a different channel, and remain-on-channel work. This is
the long-standing "monitor mode will not stay on the channel" drift seen with
airodump-ng / aireplay-ng; the drift targets track nearby APs' channels
because those are the channels the upper layers are chasing.

The driver cannot tell an explicit "iw dev X set channel N" apart from a
mac80211-initiated hop -- both are the same .config call with the same flag --
so this adds an opt-in module parameter, monitor_channel. When it is set to a
non-zero 802.11 channel number and the interface is in monitor mode,
rt2x00mac_config() clears IEEE80211_CONF_CHANGE_CHANNEL from any request whose
target channel differs from the locked one, so the radio is not retuned. Every
other part of that configuration change (TX power, retry limits, PS, and the
CONFIG_MONITORING enable/disable, which rt2x00lib_config() derives from
IEEE80211_CONF_MONITOR independently of the channel flag) is still applied.

Why this is safe:
  - The guard is gated on IEEE80211_CONF_MONITOR. Station, AP and managed
    modes never set that flag, so their channel switching (including CSA) is
    completely unaffected.
  - The default value 0 disables the feature, so existing users get identical
    behaviour to stock.
  - Link tuning and VCO calibration are not touched and keep running normally.
  - Matching is done via ieee80211_frequency_to_channel(center_freq), so it
    is correct on both the 2.4 GHz and 5 GHz bands (chan->hw_value is only an
    index into spec.channels[], not the 802.11 channel number, so it is not
    used here).

Known limitation (cosmetic): rt2x00lib_config() still copies conf->chandef
into rt2x00dev->curr_freq/curr_band unconditionally, so while a change is being
refused the per-packet radiotap channel annotation can briefly reflect the
channel mac80211 believes it moved to rather than the pinned channel. The
radio physically stays on the locked channel for both capture and injection.

Usage:
  echo 6 | sudo tee /sys/module/rt2x00lib/parameters/monitor_channel
  sudo ip link set wlan0 down
  sudo iw dev wlan0 set monitor control
  sudo ip link set wlan0 up
  sudo iw dev wlan0 set channel 6
  # radio now stays on channel 6. Set the parameter to 0 to restore stock
  # behaviour, or to another channel N (then re-issue "iw ... set channel N")
  # to move the lock.

The marker CAELION-FIX-RT3070-CHANDRIFT appears in the code comments and in
the debug message emitted when a channel change is refused.

Not compile-tested in the authoring environment; structurally validated
against current mainline sources (function signatures, flag definitions and
the ieee80211_frequency_to_channel helper were all read from the tree). If you
submit this upstream, add your own Signed-off-by: line -- I have deliberately
not added one, since the DCO sign-off must be yours.

Apply from the root of a matching kernel source tree with:  patch -p1
---
 drivers/net/wireless/ralink/rt2x00/rt2x00mac.c | 39 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 39 insertions(+)

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ralink/rt2x00/rt2x00mac.c	2026-09-29 07:19:29.582274500 -0400
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ralink/rt2x00/rt2x00mac.c	2026-09-29 07:22:21.017633100 -0400
@@ -16,6 +16,23 @@
 #include "rt2x00.h"
 #include "rt2x00lib.h"
 
+/*
+ * CAELION-FIX-RT3070-CHANDRIFT
+ * Optional monitor-mode channel lock. When set to a non-zero 802.11 channel
+ * number, the driver refuses runtime channel-change requests that would move
+ * the radio off that channel while an interface is in monitor mode. This pins
+ * a fixed-channel capture (e.g. airodump-ng/aireplay-ng on one channel)
+ * against off-channel excursions requested from above the driver (software
+ * scan, channel-context reassignment, remain-on-channel), all of which reach
+ * this hardware as IEEE80211_CONF_CHANGE_CHANNEL and are the source of the
+ * observed drift. Default 0 preserves historical behaviour; station, AP and
+ * managed modes never set IEEE80211_CONF_MONITOR and are unaffected.
+ */
+static int monitor_channel;
+module_param(monitor_channel, int, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(monitor_channel,
+		 "Lock monitor-mode radio to this 802.11 channel (0 = disabled)");
+
 static int rt2x00mac_tx_rts_cts(struct rt2x00_dev *rt2x00dev,
 				struct data_queue *queue,
 				struct sk_buff *frag_skb)
@@ -317,6 +334,28 @@
 		return 0;
 
 	/*
+	 * CAELION-FIX-RT3070-CHANDRIFT
+	 * In monitor mode with the channel lock armed, drop a channel-change
+	 * request that targets a channel other than the locked one, so the
+	 * radio stays put. Everything else in this configuration change
+	 * (power, retry limits, PS, monitor enable/disable, ...) is still
+	 * applied by rt2x00lib_config(). The lock only acts while
+	 * IEEE80211_CONF_MONITOR is set, so it cannot affect station, AP or
+	 * managed-mode channel switching.
+	 */
+	if ((changed & IEEE80211_CONF_CHANGE_CHANNEL) &&
+	    monitor_channel > 0 &&
+	    (conf->flags & IEEE80211_CONF_MONITOR) &&
+	    conf->chandef.chan &&
+	    ieee80211_frequency_to_channel(conf->chandef.chan->center_freq) !=
+		monitor_channel) {
+		rt2x00_dbg(rt2x00dev,
+			   "CAELION-FIX-RT3070-CHANDRIFT: refusing channel change to %d MHz, locked on channel %d\n",
+			   conf->chandef.chan->center_freq, monitor_channel);
+		changed &= ~IEEE80211_CONF_CHANGE_CHANNEL;
+	}
+
+	/*
 	 * Some configuration parameters (e.g. channel and antenna values) can
 	 * only be set when the radio is enabled, but do require the RX to
 	 * be off. During this period we should keep link tuning enabled,
